      "question": {
        ":question/id": "d4dc881e-d87b-51a1-ad9e-4c70d5e6ee9e",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $x\\cdot\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1$ for $x>0$?",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Rearranging the equation, we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}$.\n\nIntegrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =1\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\ln|x|+C \\\\ y & =\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2$ for $x>0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/edb-math/repairs/2026-10-27-topic-audit-2/group-3/relocation-assets/17592186096698/a/3b2f1c0c327db086f8e2977719b51501a89049d63b8b18357c6e905edb513b86.png)",
