  {
    "kp": 17592186068842,
    "level": "easy",
    "record": {
      "knowledge_point": {
        "db_id": 17592186068842,
        "id": "c0d908f1-591e-5cb6-afc5-fa7ec44ec326",
        "title": "Separating the Variables in a Differential Equation"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "d253d5b6-75bc-5f0a-9641-cb103c6aaab8",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={2}^{x}{y}^{4}\n$$\n\nWork on an open domain where all displayed expressions are defined and the dependent-variable factor is nonzero. Choose an equivalent equation in the form $A(y)\\, \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=B(x)$, with a multiplier depending only on $y$ and a right-hand side depending only on $x$.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Expose the independent-variable and dependent-variable factors:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={2}^{x}{y}^{4}={2}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{{y}^{4}}}.\n$$\n\nMultiply both sides by the reciprocal of the dependent-variable factor. On the stated domain, this gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={2}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative multiplier depends only on $y$ and the right-hand side only on $x$.",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "881ab801-9084-5e7b-afa2-8c2f8e068142",
            ":answer-field/key": "selection",
            ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
            ":answer-field/choices": [
              {
                ":answer/id": "69362b9e-93a0-50d7-92d7-2e349deabed0",
                ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
                ":answer/value": "\\frac{1}{{y}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={2}^{2x}"
              },
              {
                ":answer/id": "3f053158-ec4c-5e60-8e6a-cfba80ee33f2",
