        "title": "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "df1df115-40c0-587a-a2bf-2b5090f40518",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cooling iron can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.03(T-31)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the iron is ${102}^{\u2218}C,$ calculate its temperature after $12$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We're told that the temperature of the cooling iron is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.03(T-31).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-31} & =-0.03\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-31} & =-\\int 0.03\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|T-31| & =-0.03t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-31>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-31 & ={e}^{-0.03t+C} \\\\ T-31 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.03t} \\\\ T-31 & =K{e}^{-0.03t} \\\\ T & =31+K{e}^{-0.03t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the iron was ${102}^{\u2218}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=102,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}102 & =31+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 102 & =31+K \\\\ K & =71\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=31+71{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the iron after $t=12$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(12)=31+71{e}^{-0.03\\cdot 12}\u2248{81}^{\u2218}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "c1ed7bd9-b173-596e-b96f-5a5852635966",
            ":answer-field/key": "selection",
