        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/id": "1b524349-7799-5bb3-9a1e-49fc6be92d99",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $y=-6{e}^{-8-x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=-6{e}^{u},\\,u=-8-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-6{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-6{e}^{u}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =6{e}^{u} \\\\  & =6{e}^{-8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
