      "question": {
        ":question/id": "d291c02d-d552-546d-b65c-d3f087be5ea4",
        ":question/problem": "If $f(x)=4\\cdot {6}^{-4-8x}$, find ${f}^{\\prime}(x)$.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=4\\cdot {6}^{u},\\,u=-4-8x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=4\\cdot {6}^{u}\\ln(6),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-8.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & =4\\cdot {6}^{u}\\ln(6)\\cdot (-8) \\\\ & =(-8)\\cdot 4\\cdot {6}^{u}\\ln(6) \\\\ & =-32\\cdot {6}^{-4-8x}\\ln(6).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
